  "narrative": "I assess the 12 new results using the convention that estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction; no additional sign flip is applied. For interactions, this means the signed value measures how much the exposed-minus-comparator contrast is shifted toward the registered direction in the named subgroup relative to its complement. For the specificity probes, I judge the same-direction part of the signed interval against the outcome's typical absolute dependency-score scale; for interactions, I judge it against the corresponding main association because a clinically meaningful interaction should modify a large main effect by a nontrivial fraction.\n\nScreen-quality interactions are unsupported for all three major associations. H118, SMARCA4-loss interaction with high screen_roc_auc for dependency_SMARCA2, is +0.0148 (95% CI -0.0240 to +0.0536). The largest same-direction modification allowed by the interval is about 0.054, roughly 7% of the SMARCA2 main association near +0.78 to +0.80. That is not scientifically meaningful interaction evidence, so I reject and close. H119, KEAP1-loss interaction with high screen quality for dependency_NFE2L2, is -0.0061 (CI -0.0465 to +0.0342), again far below 10% of the NFE2L2 main effect near +0.80; reject and close. H120, MSI-high interaction with high screen quality for dependency_WRN, is -0.0004 (CI -0.0603 to +0.0595). The point estimate is essentially zero, and the same-direction upper bound is about 0.059, only about 7.5% of the WRN main effect near +0.79; reject and close. These results strengthen the view that the three largest main associations are not driven by screen_roc_auc strata. They do not prove exact homogeneity or exclude residual library/batch confounding in low-quality screens, but there is no evidence of meaningful effect modification here.\n\nThe four TMED10 probes support outcome specificity rather than generic dependency-score shifts. H121 tests MSI-high against dependency_TMED10: signed estimate -0.0163 (CI -0.0482 to +0.0156). The same-direction upper bound is only +0.0156, roughly 6% of the absolute TMED10 mean scale about 0.25, below the 10% clinical-significance benchmark. Reject. H122 tests KEAP1 loss against dependency_TMED10: -0.0015 (CI -0.0210 to +0.0181), with same-direction maximum under 10% of the outcome scale; reject. H123 tests PTEN loss against dependency_TMED10: -0.0124 (CI -0.0360 to +0.0112); reject. H124 tests TP53 loss against dependency_TMED10: +0.0015 (CI -0.0182 to +0.0212); reject. Collectively, with the earlier SMARCA4-loss/TMED10 null, these findings make it less plausible that the accepted predictors are simply shifting many dependency scores in the same direction. This supports, but does not prove, biological outcome-specificity.\n\nThe complete-matched-QC robustness results are strong. H125, SMARCA4 loss versus intact in lines lacking complete matched RNA+DNA+CRISPR QC, is +0.7916 (CI +0.7487 to +0.8346). Under direction -1, this corresponds to much stronger SMARCA2 dependency in SMARCA4-loss lines. H126, KEAP1 loss versus intact in the QC-incomplete subset, is +0.7755 (CI +0.7290 to +0.8220) for dependency_NFE2L2; under direction +1 this corresponds to higher, i.e. weaker, NFE2L2 dependency in KEAP1-loss lines. H127, MSI-high in the QC-incomplete subset, is +0.7830 (CI +0.6955 to +0.8704) for dependency_WRN under direction -1, corresponding to much stronger WRN dependency. These lower bounds remain huge and well beyond the clinical-significance threshold. I accept and close all three. H127 has only 23 MSI-high exposed cells in the QC-incomplete subset, but the effect remains extremely large and the interval is far from the 10% threshold, so acceptance is appropriate as a dataset-specific robustness result, not a generalizable mechanism.\n\nH128 tests whether the MSI-high/WRN association is merely a proxy for high mutation burden. Within mutation_burden >= 8, MSI-high versus microsatellite-stable remains +0.7746 (CI +0.7373 to +0.8120), directionally consistent with much stronger WRN dependency. I accept and close. This reduces the concern that the MSI-high/WRN association is solely driven by global mutation burden, though residual confounding by unmeasured repair pathway activity cannot be ruled out.\n\nH129, the new canonical RB1-loss probe for dependency_KIF18A, is near null: +0.0082 (CI -0.0193 to +0.0357). The point estimate is negligible and the interval is not compatible with a 10% meaningful effect in the hypothesized direction, so I reject and close. This is a negative canonical-driver probe in this dataset and does not establish biological irrelevance.\n\nI request independent validation for H3 rather than any new result. EGFR amplification versus dependency_EGFR is the highest-value borderline canonical claim: discovery H3 is signed +0.0252 (CI -0.00025 to +0.0507), suggesting slightly stronger EGFR dependency in amplified lines, with a point estimate around 10% of the absolute mean EGFR dependency scale and a CI barely crossing zero. Because many multiplicity-tested claims have already been rejected or supported by much larger effects, confirming or eliminating this borderline canonical signal is the most useful use of the remaining validation capacity.",
  "research_notes": "Round 9 appraise update. New context/robustness tests support stability of the major associations. Formal screen_roc_auc interactions for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN are all negligible in same-direction magnitude and closed rejected. TMED10 specificity controls for MSI-high, KEAP1 loss, PTEN loss, and TP53 loss are null or same-direction below 10%, supporting outcome-specificity and arguing against broad generic dependency-score shifts. QC-incomplete subset robustness is positive and very strong for SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, and MSI-high/WRN; accept and close. WRN/MSI persists within high mutation burden, reducing mutation-burden proxy concern. RB1-loss/KIF18A is null and closed. Residual unresolved issues are mainly borderline EGFR amplification/EGFR dependency and several underpowered or imprecise interaction tests. Requested validation: H3 EGFR amplification for dependency_EGFR because it is canonical, borderline, and high scientific value. Do not interpret accepted QC/TMB subset results as generalizable or causal.",
