  "narrative": "All estimates are signed to the registered hypothesis direction. H144 completes the mutation-burden complement for TP53 loss and dependency_USP7: among lower/moderate mutation-burden lines, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency by +0.337 (95% CI +0.252 to +0.423), far above the ~0.1 practical dependency-score benchmark for clinical/scientific importance. I accept and close it; this reduces concern that the USP7 effect is merely a high-mutation-burden proxy. H148 tests BRAF V600E dependency in KRAS-wild-type lines, where a driver-specific effect would be more interpretable than in the overall null. The result is unsupported for a meaningful effect: signed estimate -0.019 (CI -0.051 to +0.013). I reject and close it. H149-H152 are new modifier probes. RB1 loss has no meaningful WRN effect, TP53 loss has no meaningful WRN effect, and MYC amplification has no meaningful STAT3 effect; all intervals are near zero and exclude clinically meaningful magnitude. MYC amplification does show a nominal WRN effect: +0.033 signed (CI +0.002 to +0.064), indicating raw stronger WRN dependency, but the interval lies below the ~0.1 absolute dependency-score benchmark and cannot support scientific/clinical meaningfulness despite nominal significance. Given multiplicity across many modifier probes, I reject H150 as small and close it. The KRAS-driver-context probes are highly informative: SMARCA4 loss remains associated with very strong SMARCA2 dependency in both KRAS-mutant lines (+0.774, CI +0.733 to +0.814) and KRAS-wild-type lines (+0.788, CI +0.766 to +0.810). KEAP1 loss remains associated with very weak NFE2L2 dependency in KRAS-mutant lines (+0.805, CI +0.767 to +0.843). TP53 loss remains associated with weaker USP7 dependency in KRAS-mutant lines (+0.410, CI +0.260 to +0.561). MSI-high remains associated with strong WRN dependency in KRAS-mutant lines (+0.783, CI +0.718 to +0.849). PTEN loss remains associated with stronger STAT3 dependency in KRAS-mutant lines (+0.249, CI +0.139 to +0.358). I accept and close all of these as robust dataset-specific associations across major oncogenic context. Critical audit: eligibility and subgroup variables are measured predictors or technical/genomic filters, not derived from the outcome, so no outcome leakage is apparent; however, these are still stratified associations and could be influenced by unmodeled co-mutations or screen context. Multiplicity is handled by accepting only effects with lower confidence bounds well above the practical benchmark and rejecting small nominal effects whose upper bounds exclude that benchmark. Independent validation is not requested now because newly accepted effects are large and consistent, while the only nominal small effect is not scientifically meaningful enough to justify using a validation slot.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 11 appraise updates. TP53-loss/USP7 weaker dependency is now accepted in both high and lower/moderate mutation-burden strata; KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 and SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 remain robust across KRAS-mutant and KRAS-wild-type contexts; MSI-high/WRN and PTEN-loss/STAT3 are also robust in KRAS-mutant context. New modifier probes do not add clinically meaningful associations: BRAF in KRAS-WT, RB1/WRN, TP53/WRN, MYC/STAT3 are null, and MYC/WRN is nominally significant but too small (0.033, CI 0.002-0.064) to accept. Durable accepted dataset-specific associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency. No new validation requested; reserve slots for any future result with a clinically meaningful point estimate but ambiguous interval. Remaining lower-priority active probes include TMED10 media/MYC, research-marker F/STAT3, ERBB2 cross-driver and PIK3CA probes, and KRAS-wild-type complements for STAT3/WRN/NFE2L2/USP7 if needed for completeness. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal mechanisms or external generalizability."
}

