  "narrative": "## Current scientific synthesis\n\n### 1. New validation and due assessment\nH168 tested a mutation-burden interaction: whether TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency more strongly in high mutation-burden lines. The discovery signed interaction was +0.209 (CI +0.059 to +0.359). That point estimate exceeded the practical 0.1 dependency-unit benchmark used during this run, but the lower bound was below 0.1. The independent validation H168 returned +0.031 (adjusted CI -0.211 to +0.273), a near-null estimate with an interval compatible with no effect. The validated evidence does not replicate a clinically meaningful positive interaction. I therefore reject H168 and close it. This does not reject the main TP53-loss USP7 association; it only rejects high mutation burden as a meaningful modifier of that association.\n\nThe mutation-burden interaction program now supports context robustness rather than context dependence. WRN/MSI-high burden interaction was null (signed -0.025, CI -0.091 to +0.040). STAT3/PTEN-loss burden interaction was null (signed -0.012, CI -0.125 to +0.101). NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss burden interaction was null (signed +0.006, CI -0.040 to +0.051). SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss burden interaction was a small nominal negative effect (signed -0.054, CI -0.097 to -0.011), but the magnitude is below the practical benchmark and does not justify a reversed interaction claim.\n\n### 2. Supported dataset-specific associations\nDependency scores are signed as registered: positive signed evidence for a stronger-dependency direction corresponds to raw more negative values, while positive signed evidence for the opposite direction corresponds to weaker dependency. The accepted effect sizes are far above the run's practical benchmark and would be judged meaningful under the 10% relative-difference instruction, while rejected/negative effects had estimates well below 0.05 and intervals near zero. The strongest and independently validated dataset associations are:\n- SMARCA4 loss and much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: signed discovery 0.784 (CI 0.765 to 0.803); validation 0.800 (CI 0.768 to 0.832). This is extremely large and precise. It replicated across adenocarcinoma, TP53 wild-type and loss, ARID1A wild-type and loss, screen ROC strata, CRISPR libraries, batch, and mutation-burden strata, with no supported effect modification.\n- KEAP1 loss and much weaker NFE2L2 dependency: accepted signed effect 0.796 (CI 0.776 to 0.816); validation 0.801 (CI 0.772 to 0.829). The stronger-dependency direction was rejected. The effect is huge, outcome-specific, and robust across lineage, culture, screen-quality, KRAS, TP53, mutation-burden, library, QC, and batch contexts.\n- MSI-high status and much stronger WRN dependency: signed discovery 0.790 (CI 0.760 to 0.819); validation 0.807 (CI 0.759 to 0.855). This is very large and precise, with null interactions for lineage, culture, screen quality, mutation burden, and marker-D negative-control evidence.\n- PTEN loss and stronger STAT3 dependency: signed discovery 0.267 (CI 0.215 to 0.319); validation 0.310 (CI 0.231 to 0.388). This is a moderate but clearly meaningful dataset association, robust across TP53 status, lineage, culture, screen quality, KRAS context, mutation-burden strata, QC, and batch, with null PIK3CA, lineage, culture, screen, and burden interactions.\n- TP53 loss and weaker USP7 dependency: signed discovery 0.396 (CI 0.325 to 0.467); validation 0.395 (CI 0.293 to 0.497). This is moderately large and validated, robust across lineage, culture, screen-quality, MYC, KRAS, mutation-burden strata, QC, and batch; the high-burden positive modifier is not supported after validation.\n\n### 3. Negative or unsupported findings\nThe receptor-driver dependency hypotheses are largely unsupported in this dataset: KRAS mutation was not credibly associated with stronger KRAS dependency; BRAF V600E was not credibly associated with stronger BRAF dependency; EGFR amplification failed validation against the EGFR-dependency hypothesis; ERBB2 amplification was null for ERBB2 dependency; and cross-receptor probes were null or noncredible. Other unsupported or too-small predictors included MYC amplification for DHX9, POLR2A, RPL11, STAT3, USP7, NFE2L2, and MDM2 contexts; ARID1A loss for SMARCA2; BRCA2 loss for WRN; RB1 loss for KIF18A and WRN; TP53 loss for MDM2; media serum for KIF18A and TMED10; TMED10 for most tested genomic exposures; NF1 loss for STAT3; PIK3CA modification of PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency; and the newly tested high-mutation-burden modifier of USP7/TP53 loss. Tested arbitrary-marker controls were null for marker E with SMARCA2 and marker D with WRN, which supports internal specificity but does not prove mechanism.\n\n### 4. Unresolved uncertainty and scope\nAll conclusions are associations measured within the supplied dataset. They are not evidence of causation, therapeutic relevance, or generalizability to other lineages, screens, or libraries not represented here. Residual uncertainty remains for untested interactions and for the remaining active robustness probes: KRAS-wild-type complements, Avana-library complements, mutation-burden lower-stratum complements, and remaining arbitrary-marker specificity controls. The rejection of H168 means the current evidence argues against treating high mutation burden as a meaningful modifier of USP7/TP53-loss dependency, although a small interaction cannot be excluded. Pending marker controls, if non-null, could reduce confidence in the internal specificity of accepted claims; if null, they would strengthen it. Pending context complements could reveal that an accepted association is narrower than the pooled population, although so far the major accepted associations have replicated across many measured contexts.\n\n### 5. Next analyses and validation policy\nI deferred lower-priority TMED10 and receptor cross-probe active claims H116, H130, H132, H136, and H137 to concentrate analysis on the more informative pending items: marker specificity and context robustness for accepted associations. Next, prioritize the already active accepted-context complements: PTEN-loss STAT3 and TP53-loss USP7 KRAS-wild-type strata, MSI-high WRN and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 KRAS-wild-type strata, Avana-library complements, mutation-burden lower-stratum complements, and the arbitrary-marker controls. If these robustness probes replicate, the accepted associations become context-robust but remain dataset-specific. If any fails materially, narrow the accepted claim to the supported contexts. No new validation was requested; the remaining voluntary validation capacity is reserved for a future result with a large clinically meaningful point estimate but an ambiguous interval.\n\n### 6. Bottom line\nThe strongest scientifically meaningful associations supported by this dataset are SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 dependency reversal, MSI-high WRN dependency, PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency, and TP53-loss USP7 dependency reversal. They are large or moderate, precise, validated where tested, and robust to most measured technical, genomic, quality, library, batch, and context strata. Most interaction tests, including mutation-burden modification, receptor-driver-specific dependencies, arbitrary-marker effects, and other genomic cross-probes, are null, nonreplicated, or too small to support meaningful scientific conclusions. All conclusions remain associational and dataset-scoped.",
